      "id": 1,
      "text": "Grand Detour is where John Deere forged the first self‑scouring steel plow in 1837, forever changing American agriculture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://scientificmagz.blog/grand-detour-il-history-guide",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village’s name comes from an odd bend in the Rock River that turns north instead of its usual southwestern flow.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Detour%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Leonard Andrus of Vermont founded Grand Detour in 1835, setting the stage for its early industrial boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Detour%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Sheffield Hotel, built in 1865, once housed workers from the local plow factory and still stands as a historic inn.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Detour%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The John Deere House and Shop, now a museum, joined the National Register of Historic Places in 1966 and is run by the Deere company.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
